    detectElutionPeaks_(mt, single_mtraces);
  }
  ```
  Note: it takes `MassTrace& mt` NON-CONST. speXtract calls `epd.detectPeaks(mt, split)` with a local non-const `mt` — fine.

  CRITICAL: the single-trace overload does NOT do width_filtering=="auto" post-filtering — but neither does the vector overload! The vector overload (320-346) also just clears, loops detectElutionPeaks_, endProgress. The filtering (fixed pw and snr) happens INSIDE detectElutionPeaks_ (steps 3.1/3.2 and 3.2/3.3), which both overloads share. `filterByPeakWidth` (the "auto" path) is a separate public method that detectPeaks never calls — the note at line 32-33 says "if you want auto, call filterByPeakWidth yourself". So the user's premise "with width_filtering=off and masstrace_snr_filtering=false the cross-trace routines are unreachable" is actually even stronger than claimed: there are NO cross-trace routines in detectPeaks at all, in either overload. The vector overload is literally clear + loop of per-trace calls. No global noise, no cross-trace SNR. computeMassTraceNoise/SNR are per-trace and only invoked when mt_snr_filtering_ is true.

  2. **detectElutionPeaks_ is purely per-trace**: smoothData(mt) (SavitzkyGolay over the trace alone), findLocalExtrema(mt, win_size/2) — all per-trace. No neighbor dependence. win_size = ceil(chrom_fwhm_/scan_time) where scan_time = mt.getAverageMS1CycleTime() — per-trace. So per-trace equivalence holds EXACTLY, including order: the nested path of the patched vector overload appends children of trace i before trace i+1; the new code converts children per trace in the same order. Output order identical.

